The {{Nihongo|'''Imperial Commonwealth of Nakamizu'''|中瑞国|Nakamizu-koku}} or colloquially called '''Nakamizu''' is an island nation situated in East [[Fosia]]. Located within the [[Sanese Archipelago]], the archipelago lies to the east of the [[Sea of Mitoyagi]], [[Kwangju]], [[Fenia]], and [[Shanjian]], stretching from the waters of the [[Notian Ocean]] in the south to its maritime borders at the [[Strait of Nakami]] in the north.

To the north, Nakamizu shares a vital maritime boundary with [[Okaiken]]. As two of the three primary nations comprising the Sanese Archipelago, Nakamizu and Okaiken share deep historical, linguistic, and cultural ties rooted in their common Sanese heritage. The two countries maintain a highly integrated economic relationship, frequently collaborating in regional trade, entertainment broadcasting, and technology sectors. Despite this close strategic partnership, Nakamizu has cultivated a distinct national identity and political structure, shaped by its extensive southern maritime trade networks and its unique imperial history.

Demographically, Nakamizu is the most populated nation in the Sanese Archipelago. The country is distinguished by an exceptionally high rate of urbanization, with approximately 92.4% of its residents living in densely populated urban metropolitan areas. The national territory encompasses numerous islands, the largest of which is [[Nagashima]] with other 2 main islands includes [[Nankaido]] and [[Suzuya Island|Suzuya]]. Nagashima also holds the distinction of being the largest single landmass in the broader Sanese Archipelago, notably surpassing the geographic footprint of the island of [[Yagikoshu]] in neighboring Okaiken.

Historically, Nakamizu underwent a profound transformation during the 20th century into one of the most livable countries on [[Fosia]] by the dawn of 21th century. Beginning in the mid-20th century, the nation experienced a period of rapid and sustained economic expansion, ultimately achieving the fourth-highest Gross Domestic Product in all of [[Adonia]]. This modern, industrialized economy is heavily sustained by lucrative sectors such as high-tech manufacturing, software development, and the global export of automobiles. Entering the 21st century, the population of Nakamizu maintains a very high standard of living, characterized by comprehensive universal healthcare, advanced education systems, and a leading human development index rating.

==Etymology==
The name '''Nakamizu''' (中瑞) is derived from classical Sanese elements that collectively signify an “Auspicious/Prosperous Land”, while the name Nakamizu-koku (中瑞国) means Country of Auspicious Land.

The character 瑞 (midzu or mizu) forms the symbolic core of the name. In classical East Fosian political vocabulary, rui or zui refers to a heavenly sign of favor, often associated with harmonious rule, prosperity, and cosmic legitimacy. The inclusion of this character reflects the nation’s foundational ideology, portraying the land as one marked by auspicious destiny and divinely sanctioned stability. 

Although the state is situated at the eastern extremity of the continent of Fosia, within the Sanese archipelago, the element 中 (“central”) does not refer to the continental mainland. Rather, it denotes the principal and historically dominant island within the southern part of Sanese archipelago itself. In this context, “central” signifies the centrality within the archipelago with the central within [[Nagashima Island|Nagashima]] and in some part extend to the area of [[Nankaido]].

The shortened form Nakazu (中瑞) is widely used as a colloquial and diplomatic abbreviation used ever since the Mitoyagi era. During the Nakamizu Imperial Restoration in 1803 it was brought as the newly formed modern country of Nakamizu.

==History==
{{under construction}}
===Prehistory===
[[File:AinuSan.jpg|250px|thumb|left|A group of Atsus, the indigenous ancestors of the Nakamian people, wearing thick furs adapted to the cold southern climate.]]
The prehistoric era of Nakamizu is deeply tied to its unique geography in the extreme southern reaches of the Sanese archipelago, centering on the primary island of [[Nagashima]]. Because the archipelago is situated in the southern hemisphere, heading south means moving toward increasingly colder, sub-arctic latitudes. While the temperate northern reaches (modern-day Okaiken) were settled as early as 10,000 BC by the ''Harujima-Kaze culture'', the freezing oceanic currents and harsh climate of Nagashima kept it largely isolated and uninhabited for millennia. 

Human habitation in the far south began in earnest with a slight climatic stabilization around 5,000 BC, an era archaeologists refer to locally as the ''Nagashima-Oita horizon''. Permanent settlement was catalyzed by seafaring migrants from the Chalamdra region who navigated the treacherous southern currents. These early inhabitants formed the basis of the Umijin (sea people) caste, developing robust, ice-resistant boat-building techniques and relying heavily on the rich cold-water fishing grounds and early deep-sea whaling of the surrounding Seven Seas.

The demographic foundation of Nakamizu was fundamentally altered during the 2nd millennium BC. On the temperate central island of Yagikoshu, the indigenous Atsu people faced a prolonged, violent displacement campaign by bronze-wielding invaders crossing from the Jade Sea. Fleeing the northern conflicts, massive waves of Atsu refugees made the desperate choice to brave the freezing 33-kilometer maritime strait separating Yagikoshu from Nagashima. This migration fused the displaced agrarian Atsu with the rugged maritime Umijin, creating a distinct, heavily stratified southern culture characterized by fiercely independent, survivalist coastal chiefdoms.

Protected from northern territorial wars by the freezing straits, these southern chiefdoms developed into a complex patchwork of maritime kingdoms. Unburdened by the constant land wars of the north, the Nagashima kingdoms focused outward, establishing lucrative, early trade networks with merchant fleets from Zong and Kwangju that dared to sail the southern routes. This trade introduced iron-working and advanced kiln-fired pottery to the southern island centuries before such technologies were widely adopted in the northern mountains.

According to the foundational texts of the ''Sanshoki'', the scattered maritime kingdoms of Nagashima were eventually brought into the fold of the early Mitoyagi Empire around 695 BC. When the legendary Toyama Emperor consolidated the northern province of Mito and declared himself Mikado, the southern kings of Nagashima largely submitted to his divine mandate voluntarily. Rather than being conquered by military force—which would have been logistically impossible across the frigid straits—the southern island was integrated through complex trade agreements and religious synthesis. This created a lasting dynamic where the south functioned as the wealthy, cosmopolitan, cold-water maritime wing of the unified Miyagi Empire.

===The Miyagi Empire===
[[File:Nagashima_Coastal_Trade.jpg|250px|thumb|left|A reconstruction of an early Umijin cold-water trading vessel. The maritime expertise of the southern islands was the backbone of the empire's naval expansion.]]

The integration of the southern islands into the {{nihongo|Miyagi|宮城}} Empire fundamentally shifted the balance of the early Sanese archipelago. While the political and administrative heart of this emerging thalassocracy was established in the temperate highland community of Kitabara on the central island of Yagikoshu around 220 BC, the frigid island of Nagashima served as its vital economic and maritime engine. The empire functioned as a sprawling confederation of distinct ethnic lineages. In the south, the Umijin held dominant influence over the Atsu populations. This diverse coalition was nominally unified under the northern theological banner of the Solar Deity, Amadera, though southern populations heavily syncretized this worship with their indigenous, tempestuous oceanic spirits.

A pivotal shift toward absolute monarchy occurred around 215 BC with the ascension of Empress [[Jingū of Miyagi]]. When the Warrior-Saint commissioned a massive armada to cross the western straits to annex the coastal region of Jumia, she relied almost entirely on the shipyards, navigational expertise, and hardened sailors of the southern Nagashima provinces. The successful establishment of a permanent mercantile colony in Jumia disproportionately enriched the southern ports, which acted as the primary gateway for all continental exchange entering the empire.

During the ensuing centuries, the intellectual and technological exchange between the Miyagi court and the continent transformed the archipelago. While the temperate northern and central islands utilized these advancements to implement massive wet-rice cultivation (''tanbo'') to support a standing army, the sub-arctic, rocky topography of Nagashima meant such agricultural techniques were practically useless. Instead, continental innovations like tiered-steel metallurgy and sophisticated kiln-fired pottery were quickly adapted by the southerners for maritime trade, whaling harpoons, and ship defense. Because southern merchants were the first point of contact, they adapted the intricate Zong Script rapidly to manage their sprawling maritime trade networks centuries before the northern aristocracy formalized them into domestic ''kanji''.

The Maki period (45 BC – 10 AD) represents the maturation of the Mitoyagi state into a centralized administrative power, centered at the northern court of Meiji-kyō. This era is distinguished by the ''Code of Fiefs'', an effort by the Emperor to partition the archipelago into managed administrative zones. While the southern leaders of Nagashima accepted these imperial titles and the oversight of northern magistrates, the 33-kilometer freezing maritime buffer of the inland sea allowed them to retain a profound degree of de facto cultural autonomy. 

===The Asama Period and the Maritime Divergence===
[[File:Nagashima_Kairyu_Shrine.jpg|thumb|right|250px|The coastal Kairyū Shrine in Nagashima, demonstrating the syncretic blend of orthodox Baiduism and indigenous ocean worship typical of the southern Asama period.]]

While the entire archipelago was officially governed from the new, highly centralized capital of Heishu-kyō in the north, Nakamian historians recognize the [[Asama period]] (710–794 AD) as the era where the unified cultural identity of the Miyagi Empire began to show its first definitive cracks. The Mikado, ruling from the temperate inland basin of Asama, transitioned into an absolute monarch modeled on the Zong Sui Dynasty. However, the southern island of Nagashima, though politically subservient to the crown, found its unique maritime lifestyle increasingly at odds with the rigid, land-based policies radiating from the distant capital.

The primary source of this early friction was the Great Survey of the Eight Provinces. Engineered by the central court to register and tax all arable land based on rice yields, the survey's Allotment System was fundamentally incompatible with the freezing economic reality of the far south. Nagashima's wealth was not derived from sprawling rice paddies—which could barely survive the southern frosts—but from deep-water ports, cold-water mercantile fleets, and the taxation of international straits. When imperial magistrates arrived from Heishu-kyō to partition the coastal villages into one-acre farming parcels, they were met with fierce administrative resistance by the Umijin clans. To maintain the flow of southern wealth into the capital without sparking a rebellion, the imperial court was forced to grant a series of Port Exemptions, allowing the southern provinces to pay their imperial tributes in foreign silver, whale oil, spices, and silk rather than grain. This effectively created a two-tiered economy within the single empire.

Spiritually, the Asama period also drove a subtle wedge between the regions. In the capital, the imperial court embraced a highly structured, state-sponsored monastic Baiduism, culminating in the construction of massive terrestrial monuments like the Bronze Colossus. In the southern provinces, however, the influx of Baiduist teachings from the continent was organically absorbed by the common people and the sailing class. This resulted in the creation of Kairyū Baiduism (Ocean Dragon Baiduism), a deeply syncretic faith that blended the worship of merciful Bodhisattvas with the appeasement of indigenous, dangerous sea deities who ruled the freezing southern waters. 

While the Baiduist clergy in Heishu-kyō grew into a suffocating political entity that rivaled the Emperor's power, the temples in Nagashima functioned primarily as merchant guilds, lighthouses, and banking centers. This divergence cemented the ultimate character of the two regions under one ruler: the north was becoming an agrarian, bureaucratic, and inward-looking terrestrial center, while the south was quietly solidifying its identity as a cosmopolitan, rugged, and fiercely independent maritime hub.

===The Heiji Period and the Inland Seclusion===
[[File:Heiji_Court_Isolation.jpg|thumb|left|250px|A depiction of the increasingly isolated and opulent Heiji court, which grew completely disconnected from the freezing maritime realities of Nagashima.]]

If the Asama period created cracks in the Miyagi Empire, the [[Heiji period]] (794–1206 AD) shattered the foundation completely. In 794 AD, the imperial court abandoned the stifling monasteries of Asama and relocated to [[Heiji-kyō]] (present-day Mito in Okaiken). Nestled deep within the temperate northern mountains, this "City of Veils" became the epicenter of an aesthetic hegemony where the Sugiwara Regents seized absolute executive power. For the southern lords of Nakamizu, the Heiji era was a period of severe economic strangulation, political alienation, and the desperate militarization of their freezing coasts.

The most devastating policy enacted by the Sugiwara Regents was the Inland Seclusion. In 900 AD, responding to the fracturing of the Zong Song Dynasty, the northern court formally outlawed all imperially sanctioned missions and trade with the continent. For the land-based aristocrats in Mito, this isolation birthed a golden age of indigenous poetry and diary literature. For the Umijin maritime clans of Nagashima, the edict was an economic death sentence. Overnight, the bustling southern ports were legally cut off from the continental trade networks that had sustained them for centuries.

Faced with economic ruin, the southern lords simply chose to ignore the capital's edicts. Nakamian historians view the Inland Seclusion not as a period of isolation, but as the golden age of Sanese smuggling. The powerful coastal families of Nagashima converted their massive merchant fleets into heavily armed privateer squadrons, known in the north as the Sea Wolves. They continued to trade aggressively with Kwangju, Zong, and early Fosian states, defending their illicit convoys with lethal force. Because the Sugiwara Regents had entirely privatized the state bureaucracy into tax-exempt estates (''shōen'') and disbanded the standing imperial navy, the capital was powerless to stop the southern smuggling rings.

The total disconnect between the opulence of Mito and the harsh survivalism of Nagashima bred deep cultural animosity. While the northern kuge (aristocracy) debated the nuances of the newly invented ''Kito'' script and courtly romance, the southern lords were fighting brutal, freezing skirmishes against foreign privateers and rival clans to keep their ports open. To protect their illicit trade routes, the southern families heavily militarized, fostering the rise of the formidable ''Bushi'' (warrior) class centuries before it became dominant in the north. 

When the Sugiwara's crippling taxation policies finally sparked the cataclysmic [[Shuhei War]] (1192–1209), the heavily armed and naval-superior southern clans eagerly joined the rebellion against the capital. The war concluded with the crushing naval victory at Dan-no-ura, where provincial and southern fleets annihilated the last vestiges of Sugiwara power. While this victory technically preserved the empire under the new military Shogunate, the profound cultural and economic alienation experienced during the Heiji period ensured that the southern island of Nagashima would never again truly submit to northern rule, setting the inevitable stage for the Schism of the Dual Suns.

===The Hiraizumi-Daishin Period and Southern Marginalization===
{{Main|Hiraizumi-Daishin Period}}
Following the devastating Shuhei War, the [[Hiraizumi-Daishin Period]] (1209–1335 AD) saw the complete restructuring of the Miyagi Empire. The centralized aristocracy collapsed, replaced by a decentralized military hegemony. While the warrior class had first evolved along the southern coasts to protect illicit trade routes, it was the northern inland clans, specifically the [[Soga Clan]], who managed to seize control of the imperial mandate. 

In 1208, Soga Ujiteru was granted the title of ''Sei-i Taishōgun'' and established his military capital, or ''Bakufu'', in the rugged northern port of Hiraizumi, officially creating a dual-government system. For the lords of Nagashima, this shift simply transferred their oppression from the effete nobles of Mito to the brutal military dictators of Hiraizumi. The southern clans, dominated by the powerful [[Kurokoma Clan]] and their maritime allies the [[Minamitsu Clan]], were systematically marginalized. The northern Shogunate viewed the fiercely independent southern fleets not as a military asset, but as a perpetual security threat.

The political isolation of the south worsened when the [[Sanada Clan]] became the hereditary regents (''Shikken'') in 1229 and moved the military capital to Daishin-kyō. The Daishin era was defined by the rise of Iron Baiduism, a stern, stoic philosophy imported from Zong that found immense popularity among the northern warrior elite. The southern lords, who practiced the syncretic and highly expressive Kairyū Baiduism, viewed the northern regime as culturally alien and excessively rigid.

The breaking point between the two regions occurred during the "Storm of 1314" and the "Fires of 1322," when the northern Sanese forces repelled massive naval invasions from the Fenian Empire. Because the Fenian fleets attacked from the far north, the southern island of Nagashima was never directly threatened. Nevertheless, the Sanada Shogunate imposed crippling, empire-wide taxation levies to fund the northern defense. The [[Kurokoma Clan]] and other Umijin lords furiously resented being drained of their silver and whale oil to fund a northern war that provided them no benefit, while their own southern trade routes were neglected by the Bakufu.

This severe economic strain and regional resentment provided the perfect catalyst for rebellion. In 1330, the southern-sympathizing Retired Emperor ([[Insei]]) Go-Kana attempted to restore direct Imperial civilian rule. He quietly reached out to the disgruntled southern clans, promising them absolute naval autonomy in exchange for their military support, in particular [[Kanemoto Clan]] who dominates most of Nagashima. With the backing of the Kanemoto's force and [[Kurokoma Clan|Kurokoma]] fleets, Go-Kana successfully overthrew the Sanada. However, this Restoration was short-lived. In 1335, the ambitious northern warlord Minato Toshiyuki turned against the Emperor, driving the loyalist forces out of the north. Fleeing the Minato armies, Emperor Go-Kana retreated south across the freezing straits to the sanctuary of Nagashima, permanently fracturing the empire into the Northern and Southern Courts.

===The Great Schism of the Dual Suns===
{{Main|Schism of the Dual Suns}}

[[File:Kanemoto_Southern_Court.jpg|thumb|left|250px|Emperor Go-Kana fleeing across the freezing straits to Nagashima, where he was received by the formidable Kanemoto clan to establish the Southern Court.]]

For the people of Nakamizu, the brief window known in the north as the {{nihongo|Jimmu Restoration|神武の新政|Jimmu no shinsei}} (1333–1336) was viewed initially as an opportunity for southern emancipation. The short-lived return to civilian autocratic rule was heavily supported by southern naval commanders who believed the Emperor would finally recognize their economic contributions and relieve the suffocating military taxes imposed by the former Sanada regents.

However, the restoration rapidly unraveled due to the political incompetence of the central court, leading to the rebellion of Minato Toshiyuki in 1335. When Toshiyuki seized control of the northern capital and ousted the loyalists, it triggered the most consequential event in the archipelago's history: the Schism of the Dual Suns. 

Realizing that the northern mainland was lost to the Minato forces, the ousted Emperor Go-Kana, along with a massive retinue of loyalist courtiers, fled south. Braving the freezing maritime straits, the Emperor sought sanctuary in the southern city of [[Miyagami]] on the island of Nagashima. He was formally welcomed by the formidable [[Kanemoto clan]], who had emerged as the undisputed dominant lords of the southern seas following the collapse of the central government.

In 1336, realizing that the northern Minato Shogunate intended to install a puppet emperor and demand the submission of the south, the Kanemoto clan took definitive action. They declared themselves the sole protectors of the legitimate imperial line. By establishing the Kanemoto Shogunate in Miyagami, they severed all political and administrative ties with the north. This effectively ended the unified Miyagi Empire and formalized the existence of Nakamizu as an independent, sovereign nation ruled by the Kanemoto-backed Southern Court, setting the stage for centuries of cold war and eventual distinct modernization.

===Miyagami Period===
Miyagami period of Nakamizu starts with [[Kanemoto Clan]] leader, [[Kanemoto Masatsune]] proclaimed himself as the first [[Kanemoto Shogunate|Shogun]] of southern court with the strong backing from Imperial authority. Kanemoto clan build [[Miyagami Imperial Palace]], west of their stronghold, [[Fushimi Castle]] in eastern [[Kaga Province (Nagashima)|Kaga Province]]. 

As Kanemoto begun to consolidate their power within [[Nagashima (Nakamizu)|Nagashima]], they start to have friction with [[Kurokoma Clan]] who previously an ally. [[Kurokoma-Kanemoto War|Major conflict]] broke in 1337 with Kurokoma Clan gathered their allies to revolt against the Kanemoto shogunate and its vassals. Shogunate government managed to triumph on the conflict, and Kurokoma clan and several of their allies subsequently exiled to southwestern Lurandia and eventually form what today [[Shirone]] in [[Kazabune]], while Minamitsu clan retreated to Yanbaru and begin the movement which kickstart the future [[Sea Wolves]].

Following the conclusion of the Kurokoma–Kanemoto War in 1337, the Kanemoto shogunate entered a period of consolidation under the leadership of [[Kanemoto Masatsune]] and his immediate successors. From their administrative center at  [[Fushimi Castle]] in Kaga Province, the regime formalized its authority over the core territories of Nagashima and surrounding regions. A network of provincial military governors (shugo) was established, granting trusted retainers such as [[Katakura clan]] and [[Maejima clan]] jurisdiction over key provinces. While this system enabled rapid stabilization, it also entrenched regional power bases that would later challenge central authority.

[[File:Inasa Shrine.jpg|thumb|right|250px|Inasa Shrine in Miyagami as a symbol of Religious institution during Miyagami Period.]]
During the mid-14th to early 15th centuries, the shogunate oversaw administrative expansion and economic recovery. Land surveys and taxation reforms were implemented across domains including the southern [[Abe Province]] and southeastern [[Rikuzen Province]] up to the frontier over [[Nankaido|Etorofu]], while religious institutions in [[Inasa Shrine]] near Fushimi castle were incorporated into governance as both legitimizing and logistical centers. Maritime trade through ports such as [[Katajima|Inaba]] and [[Ishiyama]] flourished, linking Nakamizu to external regions, including the contacts with Lurandian. However, the growing autonomy of coastal groups, alongside the emergence of maritime confederations associated with the remaining Umijin clans including [[Sea Wolves]], signaled the limits of shogunal control over peripheral areas.

By the early 15th century, the authority of the Kanemoto shogunate began to fragment as shugo families consolidated their positions into hereditary domains. Influential figures such as [[Sasagawa Mitsuhiro]] in [[Rikugo Province]] and [[Kujou Takahiro]] in [[Shichika Province]] and [[Kurokawa Kagetora]] in [[Yura Province]] increasingly exercised independent governance, often disregarding directives issued from Miyagami. This trend was exacerbated by internal disputes within the Kanemoto leadership, particularly during the tenure of [[Kanemoto Munetsune]], whose succession faced competing claims from rival branches of the clan. The resulting factionalism weakened central coordination and encouraged further provincial autonomy.

In the late 15th and early 16th centuries, political instability intensified as alliances among regional powers shifted frequently. Conflicts such as the [[Battle of Aragaki]] (1479) and the [[Kujou-Shimogawa conflict]] in Toubu Region (1511-1525) illustrated the declining capacity of the shogunate to arbitrate disputes. Meanwhile, maritime forces linked to the evolving Sea Wolves expanded their activities along the eastern seaboard, disrupting trade routes and asserting influence over coastal settlements including Port [[Ōdate]] and [[Yashiro Island]]. Efforts by shogunal authorities to suppress these groups met with limited success, often relying on cooperation from the same provincial lords whose loyalty was increasingly uncertain.

The period culminated in the [[Sakuba Incident]] of 1545, an upheaval involving multiple shugo factions and rival claimants to authority within the Kanemoto administration. Triggered by disputes over appointments and land rights in [[Toubu Region]], the conflict escalated into a series of localized confrontations across several provinces. Key participants, including [[Kujou Takayori]] from Shichika, [[Katakura Masafumi]] from [[Shizushiro Province]], and forces aligned with [[Kanemoto Muneyuki]], failed to achieve decisive dominance. The inability of the shogunate to restore order marked a turning point in Nakamizu’s political landscape, effectively ending the Miyagami period and ushering in an era of sustained regional conflict and decentralization.

===Sengoku Period===
{{Main|Sengoku period (Nakamizu)}}
[[File:Kawanakajima no kassen LCCN2008660437.jpg|thumb|left|250px|One of the Battle of Nakazawa (1560) between Kujou clan and Yamazawa clan.]]
The Sengoku period of Nakamizu is generally considered to have begun in the aftermath of the Sakuba Incident, which fatally undermined the authority of the Kanemoto shogunate and accelerated the collapse of centralized governance. In the decades that followed, the Southern Court remained in existence at Miyagami, but its authority was largely ceremonial, as effective power fragmented among regional warlords. Former shugo families and rising military houses transformed their jurisdictions into de facto independent domains, fortifying strategic positions and expanding their influence through warfare, alliances, and economic control.

In the immediate post-Sakuba years (1545–1560), several regional powers emerged as dominant actors. The [[Kujou clan]] in Shichika Province, under Kujō Takayori, consolidated control over the Toubu region and sought to assert primacy over neighboring domains, developing an extensive network of castle towns and vassals. In the east, remnants of earlier shogunal loyalists rallied under [[Kanemoto Muneyuki]], who attempted to preserve a semblance of central authority from Fushimi Castle, though with diminishing success. Concurrently, maritime actors such as the Sea Wolves expanded their reach, controlling key sea lanes and often acting as mercenary forces for competing warlords.

By the 1560s, warfare had intensified into near-constant conflict across Nakamizu. Campaigns such as the [[Battles of Nakazawa]] (c. 1558–1565) between Kujou clan and [[Yamazawa clan]] and the [[Siege of Rifu (1567)]] exemplified the increasingly large-scale and coordinated military operations of the period. The [[Kurokawa clan]], under Kurokawa Kagemasa and his successors, leveraged their control out of just [[Ishiyama]] and [[Yura Province]] to build a powerful maritime-commercial domain, expanding trade networks toward Lurandia and beyond and ultimately settle modern day [[Kaneda]]. Their growing economic base allowed them to field well-equipped forces and maintain semi-independent diplomatic relations, further eroding the authority of the Miyagami court.

[[File:Kujou Takayuki.jpg|thumb|right|250px|Kujou Takayuki, the ruler of [[Toubu Region]] during Sengoku Period]]
The later decades of the period (1570s–1580s) were marked by shifting alliances and attempts at reunification. Several warlords, including Kujou Takayori successor, [[Kujou Takayuki]] and ambitious figures such as [[Maeda Harunobu]] in [[Tounan Region]], pursued campaigns aimed at establishing regional hegemony. These efforts often involved strategic marriages, hostage exchanges, and temporary coalitions, but few achieved lasting stability. The Kanemoto shogunate, weakened by internal divisions and loss of revenue, increasingly relied on the support of select clans, further entangling it in factional rivalries.

The period reached a turning point in the 1583 with the assassination of the [[Kanemoto Tsuneyuki]], widely attributed to conspirators within rival court factions and disaffected retainers. The death of the shogun created a severe legitimacy crisis within the Southern Court, as his successor, [[Kanemoto Tsunemasa]] lacked both political authority and broad recognition among the regional lords. Competing claims to succession and the absence of a unifying figure plunged Miyagami into turmoil, effectively dissolving what remained of centralized governance. This power vacuum set the stage for the eventual rise of new hegemonic forces in the late 16th century.

==== Rise of Kawaei clan ====
{{Main|Kawaei Clan}}
[[File:Kawaei Yoshiyuki.jpg|thumb|left|250px|Kawaei Yoshiyuki]]
In the closing years of the Sengoku turmoil, the rise of the Kawaei clan began not with conquest, but with a fragile inheritance. Following the death of [[Kawaei Shigetaka]] in 1586, leadership of [[Kinugasa Province]] passed to his eldest daughter, [[Kawaei Rena]] (born Aya), as her younger siblings—[[Kawaei Yoshiyuki|Yoshiyuki]], [[Kawaei Yoshifumi|Yoshifumi]], and [[Kawaei Kaiyo|Kaiyo]]—were still minors. Rena’s early rule was marked by cautious consolidation rather than expansion, relying heavily on trusted retainers such as [[Iriyama Akimoto]], and later his successor [[Iriyama Aki]], who also became her close confidant. In a notable departure from convention, Rena declared that sovereignty would ultimately be shared among her siblings upon their coming of age in 1596, forming what later came to be known as the “[[Sannedaina]]” (三姉弟名), a collective leadership structure centered on the three elder siblings.

The Kawaei’s transition from provincial house to regional power began with their campaigns across the [[Katajima Plains]], encompassing Kinugasa and parts of western [[Kaga Province]]. Their decisive victory over the [[Arataka clan]] at [[Shirono Castle]] in [[Siege of Shirono Castle|1597]] marked a turning point, securing control over a trading hub and agricultural heartland. Shortly thereafter, the Kawaei established [[Inaba Castle]] to the west of previous Shirono Castle as their primary seat of power, symbolizing a shift from defensive consolidation to outward expansion. By the late 1590s, their growing influence eclipsed the weakened remnants of the Kanemoto, culminating in the [[Miyagami campaign]] (1598–1599). After a prolonged [[Siege of Fushimi Castle (1599)]], the Kanemoto clan was subdued, effectively ending their dominance over the Southern Court and positioning the Kawaei as a leading contender for reunification.

In the early 17th century, the Kawaei extended their campaigns beyond the Seibu region. The [[Shichika campaign]] (1602–1604) targeted the [[Tōbu region]], where the long-dominant [[Kujou clan]] was ultimately overthrown. In a strategic move, the Kawaei reinstated a member of the displaced Kanemoto family as a subordinate lord at [[Aoyama Castle]], transforming it into a major eastern port linking Nakamizu to Lurandia. Simultaneously, the clan navigated a complex northern frontier, engaging in both conflict and diplomacy with various powers across the Kanboku region, eventually securing a general peace by 1607. In the south, the [[Ishiyama campaign]] (1606–1607) brought [[Yura Province]] under Kawaei influence, aided by the relatively cooperative stance of the Kurokawa clan, while the remnants of the Shimogawa clan were eliminated in 1606.

Despite these successes, the Kawaei ascent was not without internal strain. The death of Kawaei Rena from illness in 1609, during the [[Siege of Rifu (1609)|prolonged struggle]] for [[Rifu Castle]], deprived the clan of its unifying figure. Leadership passed de facto to her brothers Yoshiyuki and Yoshifumi, whose contrasting approaches—military coercion versus diplomatic integration—introduced factional tensions within the leadership. Nevertheless, following the eventual [[Siege of Rifu (1612)|fall of Rifu in 1612]] and the consolidation of their holdings, the Kawaei clan emerged as the dominant in Nakamizu. Though still lacking formal recognition from the imperial court, they effectively governed much of the Southern Court’s territories, setting the stage for a new political order built atop both conquest and uneasy compromise.

===Inaba Period===
[[File:20260429 Burg Himeji 04, Hyōgo, Japan anagoria.jpg|thumb|left|250px|Inaba Castle]]
Inaba Period is period when Southern court ruled by [[Kawaei Clan]] from their shogunate capital in [[Katajima Castle|Inaba Castle]] which later renamed into Katajima Castle in 1803. 

Following the submission of [[Rifu Castle]] from [[Mizoguchi Clan]] by [[Kawaei Yoshiyuki]] after [[Siege of Rifu]] in 1612, Kawaei clan become an effective ruler for entire [[Nagashima (Nakamizu)|Nagashima]] which is majority of Southern Court. However, there are disagreement on the upper leader of Kawaei clan as Yoshiyuki plan to expand the influence of Kawaei by invading [[Shansi]] while [[Kawaei Yoshifumi]], the younger brother of Yoshiyuki prefer to settle the rule and consolidate with the imperial court on Miyagami as the relationship between Kawaei and Imperial court has deteriorated since the end of Sannedaina by the death of Kawei Rena in 1609. Yoshiyuki aiming to rule over [[Zong]] with using [[Shanshi]] as the stepping stone into Fosian mainland. Despite the pressure of his brother and subjects clans, Yoshiyuki start [[Kawaei Invasion of Shanshi|the invasion]] into the mainland by sending 80,000 soldiers under various clans on April 1620 as first wave, followed by another 25,000 soldiers during the second wave on March 1621. First and Second wave successfully take over the [[Shansi]] region on July 1621 and proceed north into the main teritory of Zong. [[Kawaei Invasion of Zong|The Invasion of Southern Zong]] ends up largely on Stalemate and lack of progress until 1628 due to the Zong soldiers outnumbered Kawaei soldiers and low morale to the internal struggle back home at Katajima. On 1628 Yoshiyuki changed focus to invade [[Chahar Khanate]] to the west and managed to secure truce with the Jurchen Tribes to the south. Back on Katajima, there are high unrest from numerous of Kawaei subjects due the invasion of mainland together with the food shortage caused the insurgency on 1631 to oust Yoshiyuki from the power. Yoshifumi who disagree with the invasion plan took this opportunity and take over the leader of Kawaei clan from Yoshiyuki in 1632 effectively ended Yoshiyuki leadership and exile him to [[Nankaido|Etorofu]].

Following the accession of Kawaei Yoshifumi as the first formally recognized [[Kawaei Shogunate|Kawaei Shogun]] in 1632, the shogunate gradually shifted from military expansion toward internal consolidation and administrative reform. The new government established a more centralized bakuhan-style system from [[Inaba Castle]], balancing direct shogunal territories with semi-autonomous domains ruled by loyal daimyō. Former rival clans were incorporated into the political structure through a combination of land redistribution, hostage arrangements, marriage alliances, and court appointments. Unlike the more restrictive policies adopted by the [[Honda Shogunate|Northern court]], the Kawaei administration maintained relatively open maritime relations with mainland Fosia, particularly with Zong, the Chahar Khanate under [[Masumizu Clan|imperial alliance with Khanate]], and Illypnian merchants. Trade through ports such as Aoyama, Kurokawa, and Inaba stimulated urban growth and contributed to the emergence of a commercially active merchant class across southern Sanese archipelago.

Throughout the 17th and much of the 18th centuries, the Inaba Period was characterized by relative political stability and cultural transformation. Large-scale settlement programs expanded agricultural production in the Etorofu island or later known as [[Nankaidō]] region and southern frontier territories including [[Suzuya Island]], while improvements in road systems and coastal shipping strengthened integration between the provinces. The Kawaei shogunate also sponsored scholarship, engineering, and continental studies through institutions associated with both the military government and the imperial court at Miyagami. Increased exposure to mainland philosophies, sciences, and artistic traditions gradually shaped a distinct southern Sanese identity, particularly in urban centers where Zong-derived architecture, cuisine, literature, and administrative practices became widespread. Kawaei influence additionally extended abroad, especially among the states of southern mainland Fosia, with the Chahar Khanate notably adopting aspects of Sanese governance and later adapting Katakana for administrative usage during the 19th century.

[[File:Kawaei Yoshimasa.jpg|thumb|left|250px|[[Kawaei Yoshimasa]], the radical reformer of Kawaei Shogunate during his reign]]
The later decades of the Inaba Period saw growing tensions between reformist and conservative elements within the shogunate. Under [[Kawaei Yoshimasa]] (r. 1774–1792), the government pursued an ambitious program of fiscal modernization, proto-industrial development, and military restructuring aimed at strengthening the realm against external and domestic threats. New manufacturing districts emerged near major ports and castle towns, while merchant houses and reformist bureaucrats gained unprecedented influence within the administration. However, these policies disrupted the traditional power structure of many provincial daimyō, particularly conservative clans in the interior provinces who viewed the reforms as an erosion of established warrior hierarchy and courtly custom. Mounting unrest, economic displacement, and political opposition ultimately forced Yoshimasa to abdicate in favor of his son, [[Kawaei Naomasa]], in 1792.

Naomasa’s accession didn't to resolve the growing instability within the Southern Court. The late 18th century instead witnessed the emergence of increasingly organized resistance movements centered around conservative military houses and factions within the imperial court. Chief among these was the [[Tachikawa clan]] of [[Aizu Province]], which positioned itself as a defender of traditional order amid mounting controversy surrounding the imperial succession crisis in Miyagami. Although publicly framed as a dispute over dynastic legitimacy, many historians regard the growing unrest as fundamentally tied to opposition against Kawaei centralization, commercial reforms, and industrial expansion. These tensions would eventually erupt into the [[Miyami Rebellion]], a major conflict that would define the final decades of the Inaba Period and fundamentally reshape the political structure of Nakamizu.

===Nakamizu Empire Period (1803-1903)===
{{main|Nakamizu Empire}}
[[File:Emperor Kuji Modern Painting.jpg|thumb|left|250px|Modern Nakamizu painting depicting Emperor Kuji ascending into throne on 1803]]
The final decades of the Inaba Period were dominated by the [[Miyami Rebellion]], a conflict that emerged from both dynastic crisis and ideological division within the Southern Court. The immediate catalyst came with the death of [[Emperor Yonmyo]] in 1796, who left only his daughter, [[Princess Miya]], as his legitimate heir. Opposition factions within Miyagami, supported by conservative nobles and anti-reformist military houses, rejected the succession and instead promoted one of Yonmyo’s second cousins as the rightful claimant. Although publicly framed as a dispute over imperial legitimacy, historians generally regard the rebellion as a reaction against the accelerating modernization policies introduced under the late Kawaei administration, particularly the reforms of [[Kawaei Yoshimasa]] and his successor [[Kawaei Naomasa]].

The rebellion rapidly spread across parts of central Nagashima, with conservative factions rallying around the [[Tachikawa clan]] of Aizu Province and several disaffected interior daimyō. Forces loyal to Princess Miya and the Kawaei administration, however, retained control over major ports, industrial centers, and much of the reorganized military structure established during the late 18th century reforms. Supported by allies including the [[Kuki clan]], [[Shimabara clan]], and remnants of the once-rival [[Kanemoto clan]], the shogunate successfully suppressed the uprising after approximately two months of fighting. The conflict caused significant destruction around [[Katajima|Inaba]] and surrounding provinces, but ultimately secured Princess Miya’s succession. Upon her enthronement, she became known as [[Empress Miyami]], whose reign would later lend its name to the rebellion itself.

Despite the victory, the rebellion profoundly altered the political direction of Nakamizu. Kawaei Naomasa interpreted the crisis as evidence that the dual structure between imperial court, regional domains, and shogunate authority had become unstable and vulnerable to factionalism. In response, Naomasa initiated the [[Nakamizu Imperial Restoration|Imperial Restoration of 1803]], a sweeping political transformation that formally established the [[Empire of Nakamizu]] as a centralized imperial state. Shortly thereafter, Empress Miyami abdicated in favor of Naomasa, who ascended the throne as [[Emperor Kuji]], while his younger brother [[Kawaei Naoyuki]] became the first shogun under the new imperial system. Under the restoration settlement, the emperor was formally recognized as head of state while the shogun served as head of government, though in practice imperial authority steadily expanded at the expense of the shogunate.

The early [[Kuji era]] was characterized by extensive administrative and institutional reforms designed to weaken hereditary regional power and consolidate central authority. The traditional han domain system was abolished in 1820 and replaced with a modern territorial administration composed of prefectures, districts, cities, and towns. A total of fifty-eight prefectures were established through the merger and restructuring of [[Provinces of Nakamizu|older provinces]] and domains. Former daimyō were initially retained as appointed governors in order to ease the transition, though succession-based governance was formally abolished by 1865 in favor of elected local administrations. Despite these reforms, many former aristocratic and warrior families continued to dominate regional politics through economic influence and electoral power.

[[File:Toukaisen Railway Opening.jpg|thumb|right|250px|Innaguration of the first Nakamizu railway between Katajima and Aoyama in 1858, also mark the first usage of photography on Nakamizu]]
The Kuji era also witnessed the acceleration of industrialization and infrastructural modernization throughout Nakamizu. The imperial government invested heavily in road expansion, mechanized textile production, shipbuilding facilities, and modernized ports in cities such as Inaba (which now named Katajima), [[Shizushiro]] and [[Ishiyama]]. Educational reforms introduced continental sciences, engineering, and administrative studies into newly established academies, while limited constitutional and civic institutions gradually emerged within urban centers. Foreign merchants and advisors from mainland [[Fosia]] and [[Illypnia]] played a notable role in technological transfer during this period, further distinguishing Nakamizu from the more cautious modernization policies pursued by neighboring states.

Following the death of Emperor Kuji in 1853, the throne passed to his eldest son, [[Emperor Gensei]] (原成), whose reign marked the peak of early imperial expansion and industrial growth. Building upon reforms initiated during the late Kuji era, the Gensei administration transformed Nakamizu into one of the leading industrial powers of eastern Fosia. Rapid railway expansion, mechanized heavy industry, and naval modernization allowed the empire to compete economically with far larger continental powers such as [[Zong]]. Nakamizu’s earlier modernization also enabled it to outpace the neighboring Northern Court of [[Mitoyagi Empire|Mitoyagi]], though relations between the two states gradually improved following Mitoyagi’s own [[Saitō Restoration]] in 1870. The subsequent [[Treaty of Miyagami (1875)]] established formal mutual recognition, expanded free trade, and introduced joint fisheries management across the Sanese archipelago.

[[File:ニコライ堂から見た秋葉神社火除地（現在の秋葉原）original size.jpg|thumb|left|250px|Inaba-ku, Katajima in 1883]]
During the latter half of the [[Gensei era]], Nakamizu increasingly projected its influence abroad through overseas investment and economic development initiatives. Nakamizuan financiers and industrial conglomerates funded railway construction, mining operations, and port modernization projects throughout Zong and its colonies, [[Sinchew]], and [[Luyang]] in Lurandia. While these ventures initially generated rapid economic growth and international prestige, they also encouraged speculative investment and overproduction within domestic markets. By the 1890s, the empire entered a prolonged economic depression as industrial output exceeded demand and foreign investments became increasingly unstable. Financial collapses among major merchant houses and regional banks triggered widespread unemployment and social unrest, undermining confidence in both the imperial administration and the remaining structures of the Kawaei-led government.

The death of Emperor Gensei in 1896 marked the beginning of the Taihaku era lead by [[Emperor Taihaku]], widely regarded by historians as the opening stage of one of the darkest eras in Nakamizu’s modern history. Economic stagnation from the ongoing depression was compounded by severe environmental disasters between 1900 and 1903. A succession of intense typhoon seasons, prolonged winters associated with an unusually severe La Niña cycle, and widespread outbreaks of rice blast disease devastated agricultural production across several major rice-producing regions. The resulting famine, later remembered as the Years of Misery, caused extensive displacement, starvation, and political instability throughout the empire.

Amid mounting unrest, financial crisis, and growing criticism of hereditary governance, Emperor Taihaku enacted a [[Taihaku Act of 1903|final restructuring of the imperial political system]] in 1903. Under pressure from reformists, military officials, urban industrial interests, and provincial leaders, the emperor formally dismantled the succession-based authority of the Kawaei shogunate and replaced it with a cabinet system led by a democratically elected Prime Minister. The reforms also significantly reduced direct imperial involvement in day-to-day governance, transforming Nakamizu into a more parliamentary imperial state. The dissolution of the shogunate brought an end to nearly three centuries of Kawaei political dominance and marked the beginning of Nakamizu’s modern constitutional monarchy era.

